ool>\n````\n\nThe Worldmaker's Codex is live. Each of the 49 chapters gets its own particle storm, plus a web view of all the books and a view for each single book. Each storm breaks apart and reforms into that chapter's emblem: a DNA helix, a coiling dragon, a concentric castle, a lighthouse with a sweeping beam, the Ars Magica grid of techniques and forms, a laurel of 200 artists, and so on.\n\n**Graphics**\n- Three.js r178 runs on WebGPU. A TSL compute shader moves 131k particles every frame with springs, turbulence, swirl and a breathing pulse.\n- Bloom post-processing and a slowly drifting noise background make the particles glow.\n- Your pointer pushes particles away, so you can stir the storm.\n- If WebGPU isn't available, it falls back to WebGL 2 with 49k particles. The same TSL shader code compiles for both.\n- I used r178 instead of your r128 baseline, because TSL and WebGPU only exist in newer builds.\n\n**Read-aloud**\n- You can choose any voice installed in your browser, and set the speed and pitch. Your choices are remembered.\n- Each word lights up in the page text as it's spoken. Book badges glow when their titles are read.\n- Every spoken word sends a pulse through the storm.\n- With \"Keep reading\" on, it moves through the whole codex, introducing each part as it reaches it.\n\n**Navigation**\n- The left panel lists all 11 parts and 49 chapters.\n- Clicking a book badge shows a storm of just that book and what it feeds, and marks those chapters in the list.\n- Arrow keys move between chapters, and the space bar starts or pauses reading.\n\n**Workshop panel (your standard build stack)**\n- Two recorders, both trying AAC/MP4 first: one records the 3D view, the other records your screen.\n- A mixer that fits a video clip to an MP3 by looping, ping-pong, shuffle-cut or speed-ramp, so the video and music end together.\n- A gallery stored in your browser where you can play, download and delete clips.\n- A telemetry panel showing your graphics card's details and limits, plus running totals with per-minute and per-hour rates.\n\n**Limits**\n- The 3D-view recorder can't capture the browser's speech voices. To get narration on video, use the screen recorder and share the tab's audio.\n- Some embedded views block screen capture. If it's refused, open the artifact in its own tab.\n- I checked the code against the actual r178 library and test-ran all 81 storm shapes. I couldn't watch it render on a real graphics card, so if anything looks off, send a screenshot and I'll fix it.\n\n---\n\n## Turn 5: Aaron\n\nA 60 second default tuneable length of cycle recording and then lets tour ai through the walkthrough and show it for each to fit in that timeline saving mp3 audio background and mp4 video of screen capture with a generated and download list supported by browser storage - Use Frame Forge and Tarot cards as last good formatting of threejs code examples..\n\n---\n\n## Turn 6: Claude\n\n**Tool calls (16)**\n\n````text\n\u003ctool name=\"memory_read\">\n\u003cparameter name=\"path\">['/topics/past-builds.md', '/areas/integrated-open-worlds.md', '/topics/recent-work.md']\u003c/parameter>\n\u003c/tool>\n\u003ctool name=\"conversation_search\">\n\u003cparameter name=\"query\">FrameForge timeline\u003c/parameter>\n\u003cparameter name=\"max_results\">5\u003c/parameter>\n\u003c/tool>\n\u003ctool name=\"conversation_search\">\n\u003cparameter name=\"query\">Tarot cards three.js\u003c/parameter>\n\u003cparameter name=\"max_results\">5\u003c/parameter>\n\u003c/tool>\n\u003ctool name=\"conversation_search\">\n\u003cparameter name=\"query\">FrameForge MP4 recorder\u003c/parameter>\n\u003cparameter name=\"max_results\">5\u003c/parameter>\n\u003c/tool>\n\u003ctool name=\"conversation_search\">\n\u003cparameter name=\"query\">FrameForge6\u003c/parameter>\n\u003cparameter name=\"max_results\">4\u003c/parameter>\n\u003c/tool>\n\u003ctool name=\"bash_tool\">\n\u003cparameter name=\"command\">cd /mnt/user-data/outputs/artifacts/_contract/0